# Secure input, network & crypto

**Mental model:** Validate with **allowlists / schemas / bounds**; authenticate ≠ authorize; crypto and comparisons must be constant-time where secrets are involved.

## Input & object binding

- Prefer explicit typed models (e.g. Pydantic Settings/leaf configs) over `setattr` loops over arbitrary dict keys (mass assignment).
- Env/config: only via central **`Settings`** (`security.mdc`) — no scattered `os.environ` in adapters.

## Network (SSRF-shaped risks)

When code fetches a URL influenced by config, portal HTML, or operator input:

- Allowlist schemes (`https` preferred) and hosts where practical.
- Set timeouts; do not follow redirects to arbitrary internal IPs without checks.
- Scrape target URLs should live in adapter URL modules / Settings — not free-form strings from untrusted rows without validation.

## AuthZ (when APIs/UI exist)

Checking “logged in” is not enough — scope queries to the caller’s resources (IDOR). Batch scrapers without end-user tenancy still must not expose admin DuckDB paths or MFT credentials via debug endpoints.

## Crypto & secrets

- Tokens/secrets: `secrets` module — not `random`.
- Compare secrets with `hmac.compare_digest`, not `==`.
- Passwords (if ever stored): memory-hard hash (Argon2id/bcrypt) — never bare MD5/SHA for passwords.
- No hardcoded API keys; load via Settings / secret store (`security.mdc`).

## ReDoS

Avoid nested quantifiers on untrusted strings; bound input length before heavy regex. Prefer simple patterns for parsing portal text.

## Logging

Do not log secrets, tokens, or full request/SQL payloads. Be deliberate with person-level scrape fields at `info` (`security.mdc` PII note; `logging_and_errors.mdc`). Sanitize newlines if logging attacker-controlled strings.

## Checklist (before merge)

Input validated/allowlisted · SQL/paths/shell safe · timeouts on network · fail closed · no pickle/eval · secrets not in repo/logs.

